 Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix in the General Case" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050857 #edb/ref 1033 1965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050857 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6c2586fe-2403-5a67-b39e-84481a2f1fa7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123020 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "52e546ee-92c8-59a0-89c6-8d948fa6d74d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186123074 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "045c85dd-d353-50e4-ab41-50edb176bd50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124333 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124333 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5e/5ef5d9de719614493e56813f81386090eaf2329bc660ed8b4c1972080e1662f3.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124333 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a1068af3-0424-51ae-8567-7e1503209d2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124333 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_2=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](images/cc/cca1254dd52432ccefc2cfe620c078494ae5f04f02f2f321beec36eef5ffb6fd.svg)\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n![](images/bf/bfd00862d6d2d24221b11a4c5bf63baf21ab886cb1007b3164700d03eaf741bd.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124334 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124334 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8be3045e233d200bb47b1cb936536b16090333f5633517b527fc2654ad72c1c1.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e82a8038-a2fa-547d-b3ec-32e8f0e4babd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124334 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 14 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-10{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -15{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 8 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -15 & 12 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac 4 5x_2-\\dfrac35x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/8f/8fe7b5011746598e20cd72daa2ab9eb1d2db1bd51a8152c7e525d98352195001.svg)\n\nTherefore, the required basis is \n![](images/b1/b19ae8e23690c1f1f44b5efc13f175a76cec1ff1e3e9f7bc2f1fabe9f55bd554.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124335 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124335 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5f/5f07eb58825c6187ef56bc271015663abbe19b2501907c21334199b1e6d71c78.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. What are the dimensions of the eigenspaces $V_{0}$ and $V_{-2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues $\\lambda=0$ and $\\lambda=-2$ of $A$, respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124335 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f15ec22-8a8e-5547-9aa1-7ffa8b1555a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124335 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+3)=0 \\\\ -(\\lambda +2)({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda_1=0$ (simple root) and $\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=-2$ (double root). \n\nLet's now consider the eigenspaces corresponding to each eigenvector:\n\n- First, we consider $\\lambda=0.$ Since $V_{0}$ corresponds to a simple root, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(V_{0})=1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we consider $\\lambda=-2,$ which is a repeated root of the characteristic equation. To find the dimension of $V_{-2},$ we row-reduce the matrix $(A - (-2) I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(V_{-2})=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124336 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124336 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aaab6cbc7c79999778438e8eda617de9e2f83f687e5a1f09a91723ef9bcbefc3.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2$ shown above.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4,$ what is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124336 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "483a0b64-adfa-50b7-a738-2cdc135b1163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124336 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -12 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ and $x_3$ are free variables. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=2x_2-\\dfrac{5}{3}x_3.$ \n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/3f/3f1806eff823455bca7d446fb5e70b918d5d36aecda08424a8902ad16ed8a66c.svg)\n\nWe now deduce the eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2\\mathbin :$\n\n- Since ![](images/c9/c9309f0609a554365a94afbe2cc1b9e981d30e77c196189dbe44436253b1d9dc.svg) we must have that $x_2=1$ and $x_3=0.$ As a result, $a=2(1)-\\dfrac 53(0)=2.$\n\n- Since ![](images/fd/fd6445d0847b692c8d1090f0489372ce2960bf69bcab6b1b20c35f35491470cc.svg) we must have that $x_2=0$ and $x_3=3.$ As a result, $b=2(0)-\\dfrac53(3)=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=2+(-5)=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124524 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124525 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360695 #edb/ref 175921